.TH I2CHID 8
.SH NAME
i2chid \- HID devices on I²C: touchpads, touchscreens, keyboards
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B aux/i2chid
[
.B -dn
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I I2chid
drives the HID devices of a laptop that are no USB devices but
on an I²C bus (Microsoft's HID over I²C, version 1.0).
The firmware's ACPI names them: devices with the id
.B PNP0C50
(their
.B _HID
or
.BR _CID ),
present by
.BR _STA ,
their bus and address from
.B _CRS
.RB ( I2cSerialBus ),
their interrupt, a GPIO pin
.RB ( GpioInt ,
see
.IR gpio (3);
without one they are polled),
the register of the HID descriptor from
.BR _DSM .
.I I2chid
asks
.IR acpi (8)'s
interpreter
.RB ( /dev/aml ,
or
.B /srv/acpi
mounted there).
It powers each device on, resets it, reads its report descriptor and
then an input report each time its pin signals,
through
.IR i2c (3)
.RB ( #J/ bus / i2c. addr .data ).
The reports become what
.IR nusb (4)'s
.I kb
makes of a USB device's: mouse events on
.B /dev/mousein
and scan codes on
.B /dev/kbin
(no repeat yet).
.PP
A precision touchpad (Microsoft's: an Input Mode feature) is put into its
touchpad mode and reports its fingers; then
one finger moves the pointer (faster the faster it moves),
two fingers moved up or down scroll (buttons 8 and 16 of
.IR mouse (3)),
the pad pressed with one, two or three fingers on it is button 1, 3 or 2
(a thumb resting in the pad's bottom fifth not counted: pressed with it
while another finger drags is button 1),
and a short tap with as many fingers clicks the same buttons;
a touch right after a one-finger tap drags with button 1 held until the
finger goes.
A contact the pad takes for a palm counts for nothing.
Other touchpads stay in their mouse mode.
.PP
.B -d
prints the devices found and, repeated, every report.
.B -n
scrolls as on a phone: the fingers move the page.
.SH FILES
.TF /dev/mousein
.TP
.B /dev/aml
.TP
.B #γ/gpio/event
.TP
.B /dev/mousein
.TP
.B /dev/kbin
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/cmd/aux/i2chid
.br
.B /sys/src/cmd/nusb/kb/hid.c
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR acpi (8),
.IR gpio (3),
.IR i2c (3),
.IR nusb (4)
.SH HISTORY
Written for nfront; the protocol's steps as OpenBSD's
.IR ihidev (4),
the touchpad's mode and reports as its
.IR imt (4).
